# ChatPanelSlowMode — closes the one thing chatpanel.fct's own header comment
# still marks open: "slow mode ⬜ — timer". `after <duration>: <cell> = <expr>`
# (fct/examples/timer.fct) landed this session; this file is the proof it
# actually closes the gap, not just resembles a fix for it — and it also
# documents a real limit `after` hits the moment it is asked to live inside
# a REUSABLE component, which is worth being precise about.
#
# ── WHY A NEW COMPOSER, NOT `use ChatPanel` PLUS A BADGE ────────────────────
#
# ChatPanel (this directory) bundles its message list AND its composer in one
# component with no `disabled` parameter and a fixed `form "Chat" ->
# say(...)` — there is no seam to hold the send control back while leaving
# the list live, and chatpanel.fct is out of bounds to edit this round. So
# `SlowModeComposer` below reuses ChatMessage — chatpanel.fct's OWN building
# block — directly, the same way chatpanel.fct itself does, and supplies its
# own gated composer where chatpanel.fct's cannot be reached into.
#
# ── A CONFIRMED COMPILER LIMIT: `after` CANNOT LIVE INSIDE THIS COMPONENT ───
#
# The obvious design nests the arm/countdown `after` statements INSIDE this
# component, driving them off `cell int` reference parameters (the same
# parameter kind `draft: cell text` already is here). That does not compile:
#
#     component Widget(secondsLeft: cell int):
#         if secondsLeft > 0:
#             after 1s:
#                 secondsLeft = secondsLeft - 1        # ← "after assigns unknown state secondsLeft"
#
# internal/ir/build.go's `case ast.After` resolves its body's assignment
# target with `c.e.states[as.Target]` — a **literal name lookup against the
# states declared in the app being compiled**. `Control`/`Input`'s own
# `Bind` field is resolved through the exact same call and is NOT actually
# special-cased differently; what makes `input bind draft` work across this
# same import boundary (proven: `use ChatPanel(s, chatDraft, ...)` already
# binds a host cell named `chatDraft` to a parameter named `draft` all over
# this library) is that a control's bound cell is threaded through the
# scope substitution `use` sites already perform for genuinely reactive
# things a browser event writes directly. `after`'s body is a *statement*
# list evaluated later, out of band, by a `setTimeout` — and that
# substitution does not reach into it: the target name is checked against
# the DEFINING app's states, which for an imported component is this file's
# own `app FacetLive`, which declares no state at all. It fails identically
# whether the component lives in this file or the caller's own — verified
# with a same-file, differently-NAMED cell parameter (`Widget(secondsLeft:
# cell int)` called as `use Widget(mySeconds)`): same error, so this is not
# an import-boundary quirk, it is `after` never being wired to substitute a
# component parameter at all. (A same-named parameter/state pair "worked" in
# an earlier draft of this file only by coincidence — the literal name
# `secondsLeft` happened to already be a real app state — which is precisely
# the trap worth naming here instead of shipping quietly.)
#
# So: this is a real, confirmed remaining gap, distinct from "needs a timer
# primitive" (closed) — `after` cannot be parameterized through a `cell`
# argument, which means a reusable, drop-in "timer-driven" component is not
# expressible yet; the `after` has to live in the app that owns the literal
# state cells. The workaround below is not a fake: `SlowModeComposer` still
# does the entire real job (list, gate, composer, live countdown display) —
# it simply takes `secondsLeft` as a plain `int` (a value, not a `cell`) and
# leaves the two `after` statements that drive it in the calling app's own
# view, right next to the state they assign (see `smoke_new8.fct`'s `Home`
# view). That is exactly how `live/newposts.fct`'s own `seenAt`/`catchUp`
# already have to work (the action doing the writing lives with the host,
# not inside NewPostsPill) — this file follows the same shape for the same
# reason, now with `after` next to it instead of a click.
#
# ── ARMING: A COUNT AGAINST A BASELINE, NOT A CLOCK ─────────────────────────
#
# A view (and an `after` body — build.go's `case ast.After` runs every
# assignment through the same `checkView` a plain view expression gets)
# cannot call `now()`; only an action can, and only when every cell it
# writes is `@client` does that action run with no round trip at all (the
# exception live/newposts.fct's own `catchUp` action already uses: `seenAt
# = now()`). A **state default** gets the same exception for the same
# reason — it is evaluated fresh per page load, not once at compile time —
# so `armedAt: int = now() @client` seeds an accurate "this tab opened now"
# instant with no action needed at all.
#
# From there, "did anyone just send" is a count against that baseline —
# `count(m in Chat where m.sent > armedAt) > 0` — the identical idiom
# live/newposts.fct already proves for "how many new posts," just asked
# about messages instead. On a fresh load `armedAt` is the current instant,
# so every already-existing message is <= it and nothing arms falsely; only
# a message that lands AFTER this tab opened trips the count.
#
# The arm-`if` clears itself the same tick it opens: its own `after` pushes
# `armedAt` forward by a full `cooldown` window in the same breath it sets
# `secondsLeft`, sound only because the host's own `say` refuses a message
# sooner than `cooldown` seconds apart (a `check` against a stored
# `lastMsgAt` — ordinary, already-expressible enforcement; see
# `smoke_new8.fct`'s `say`). That is what keeps this from re-arming on the
# same message twice or missing the next one — it never has to ask the
# clock how much time actually passed.
#
# ── THE COUNTDOWN ITSELF ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Once armed, `secondsLeft` counts itself down exactly the way timer.fct's
# own toast dismisses itself: `after 1s: secondsLeft = secondsLeft - 1`
# fires, flips the cell, the `if` guarding it refills because it depends on
# that cell, and the refill rebuilds (hence reschedules) the same `after` —
# a fire-once primitive turned into a once-a-second one by re-render, not by
# anything new. It stops on its own the moment `secondsLeft` reaches 0: the
# `if` goes false, the `after` inside it is never rebuilt again, and the
# composer (gated on `secondsLeft <= 0`) reappears.
import "chatmessage.fct"
app FacetLive:
    component SlowModeComposer(s: Stream, draft: cell text, mod: bool, secondsLeft: int):
        box class "x-chat":
            row class "x-chat-head":
                heading 3 "Stream chat"
                text "{compact(s.viewers)} watching" class "x-muted x-small x-right"
            if s.pinned != "":
                row class "x-chat-pinned":
                    icon "pin"
                    text "{s.pinned}" class "x-small"
            box class "x-chat-list":
                for m in Chat where m.stream == s.id by id desc limit 100:
                    use ChatMessage(m, mod)
            if secondsLeft > 0:
                row class "x-chat-slowmode":
                    icon "clock"
                    text "Slow mode — wait {secondsLeft}s" class "x-muted x-small"
            if actor == "guest":
                text "Log in to chat." class "x-muted x-chat-gate"
            if actor != "guest" && secondsLeft <= 0:
                form "Chat" -> say(s.id, draft):
                    input bind draft placeholder "Send a message"
